    "global index": "GeoBench_1367",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following best describes Weber's least cost theory?\n\nOptions: \nA. The weight of the raw materials and the finished product will determine the location of the production facility.\nB. A weight-gaining industry’s production point will need to be located closer to the raw materials.\nC. A weight-reducing industry’s production point will need to be located closer to the market.\nD. Variable costs fluctuate based on the volume of the order.\nE. Fixed costs do not fluctuate based on the quantity ordered.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **A**.\n\nAlfred Weber’s least cost theory posits that the location of a manufacturing establishment is determined by the minimization of three critical expenses: transportation, labor, and agglomeration. Central to this theory is the \"material index,\" which dictates that the weight of raw materials versus the finished product determines whether a factory should be closer to the source or the market to minimize transport costs. Options B and C are incorrect because they reverse the standard logic of weight-gaining and weight-reducing industries.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
