    "global index": "GeoBench_1377",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following best explains the bid-rent theory in the United States?\n\nOptions: \nA. Wealthier families typically live within the central business district.\nB. Only commercial landlords can afford the land within the central business district.\nC. Shopping malls attract more customers than stand-alone stores.\nD. Homes in the city are usually large and expensive.\nE. It is more cost-effective to rent when living in the city.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **B**.\n\nBid-rent theory states that the price and demand for real estate change as the distance from the central business district (CBD) increases. Because land in the CBD is the most accessible and has the highest utility for commerce, commercial landlords and retailers are the only ones willing and able to pay the premium prices to occupy that space. Residential users typically move further away from the city center where land is cheaper, allowing for larger plots.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
