    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A geographer who supports the human capital model of migration would most strongly agree with which of the following statements?\n\nOptions: \nA. People's odds of migrating must be evaluated by looking at their social and cultural perspectives.\nB. Migration occurs in conjunction with a demographic transition, which encourages migration to areas of economic opportunity.\nC. As people age, their personal wealth allows them greater freedom to relocate long distances, thus spurring migration.\nD. Economic downturns force people to leave an area in search of new employment.\nE. People primarily seek to improve their incomes over the course of their lives.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The human capital model of migration treats the decision to move as an investment in oneself, where individuals calculate the costs and benefits to maximize their lifetime earnings. Unlike models that focus on demographic transitions or forced push factors, this perspective emphasizes personal agency in seeking higher wages and better economic utility. Therefore, the statement that people move primarily to improve their income over their lifespan aligns best with this model.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
