    "global index": "MATH_150",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem and provide the final answer.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For every positive integer $n$, let $\\text{{mod}}_5 (n)$ be the remainder obtained when $n$ is divided by 5. Define a function $f: \\{{0,1,2,3,\\dots\\}} \\times \\{{0,1,2,3,4\\}} \\to \\{{0,1,2,3,4\\}}$ recursively as follows:\n\n\\[f(i,j) = \\begin{{cases}}\\text{{mod}}_5 (j+1) & \\text{{ if }} i = 0 \\text{{ and }} 0 \\le j \\le 4 \\text{{,}}\\\\ f(i-1,1) & \\text{{ if }} i \\ge 1 \\text{{ and }} j = 0 \\text{{, and}} \\\\ f(i-1, f(i,j-1)) & \\text{{ if }} i \\ge 1 \\text{{ and }} 1 \\le j \\le 4. \\end{{cases}}\\]What is $f(2015,2)$?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find $f(2015, 2)$, we first analyze the behavior of the function $f(i, j)$ for small values of $i$:\n1. For $i=0$: $f(0, j) = (j+1) \\pmod 5$.\n2. For $i=1$: $f(1, 0) = f(0, 1) = 2$. Then $f(1, 1) = f(0, f(1, 0)) = f(0, 2) = 3$. Continuing this, $f(1, j) = (j+2) \\pmod 5$.\n3. For $i=2$: $f(2, 0) = f(1, 1) = 3$. Then $f(2, 1) = f(1, f(2, 0)) = f(1, 3) = 0$. Then $f(2, 2) = f(1, f(2, 1)) = f(1, 0) = 2$. Then $f(2, 3) = f(1, 2) = 4$ and $f(2, 4) = f(1, 4) = 1$.\n   The results for $i=2$ are $\\{3, 0, 2, 4, 1\\}$, which is $f(2, j) = (2j+3) \\pmod 5$.\n4. For $i=3$: $f(3, 0) = f(2, 1) = 0$. Then $f(3, 1) = f(2, f(3, 0)) = f(2, 0) = 3$. Then $f(3, 2) = f(2, 3) = 4$. Then $f(3, 3) = f(2, 4) = 1$ and $f(3, 4) = f(2, 1) = 0$.\n   Notice that $f(3, 0) = 0$, and for $j \\ge 1$, $f(3, j) = f(2, f(3, j-1))$. Since $f(2, 0) = 3$, $f(2, 3) = 4$, $f(2, 4) = 1$, and $f(2, 1) = 0$, the sequence $f(3, j)$ becomes $0, 3, 4, 1, 0, \\dots$ which repeats every 4 steps.\n5. For $i \\ge 3$: $f(i, 0) = f(i-1, 1)$. Since $f(3, 1) = 3$, $f(4, 0) = f(3, 1) = 3$. Then $f(4, 1) = f(3, 3) = 1$, $f(4, 2) = f(3, 1) = 3$.\nAs the outputs of $f$ are always within $\\{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\}$, we observe the pattern $f(i, 2)$ for $i \\ge 3$: $f(3, 2) = 4$, $f(4, 2) = 3$, $f(5, 2) = 4$, and so on. For $i \\ge 3$, $f(i, 2) = 4$ if $i$ is odd and $3$ if $i$ is even. Since 2015 is odd, $f(2015, 2) = 4$.\n\n\\boxed{4}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 297,
        "output_tokens": 840,
        "total_tokens": 1137
      },
