      "generated_answer": "The linear congruence $8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}$ is a first-degree equation in the form $ax \\equiv b \\pmod{m}$. This congruence has a solution if and only if the greatest common divisor of $a$ and $p$ divides $b$, i.e., $\\gcd(8, p) \\mid 1$. Since the only divisor of $1$ is $1$, the congruence has no solutions if and only if $\\gcd(8, p) > 1$.\n\nGiven that $p$ is a prime number and the prime factorization of $8$ is $2^3$, the condition $\\gcd(8, p) > 1$ is satisfied if and only if $p = 2$. For any other prime $p$, $\\gcd(8, p) = 1$, and a unique solution exists (specifically, $x \\equiv 8^{-1} \\pmod{p}$).\n\nThus, the only prime $p$ for which the congruence has no solutions is $p = 2$. The sum of all such primes is simply $2$.\n\n\\boxed{2}",
