    "global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3196",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An eager AP Biology student interested in studying osmosis and the movement of water in solutions took a dialysis bag containing a 0.5 M solution and placed it into a beaker containing a 0.6 M solution. If this bag were instead placed into a beaker of distilled water, what would be the expected result?\n\nOptions: \nA. The bag will expand and eventually burst due to the pressure of water flow.\nB. The solute will flow into the dialysis bag from the beaker.\nC. The bag will remain the exact same size because no water will move at all.\nD. The bag will shrink in size and the beaker will overflow due to the movement of water.\nE. The solute will flow out of the dialysis bag into the beaker.\nF. The water will evaporate from the bag, leaving only the solute.\nG. There will be a net flow of water into the bag, causing it to swell in size.\nH. There will be a net flow of water out of the beaker, causing it to decrease in size.\nI. The water in the bag will freeze due to the change in environment.\nJ. There will be a net flow of water out of the bag, causing it to decrease in size.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is determined by the principles of osmosis, which states that water moves from an area of lower solute concentration (hypotonic) to an area of higher solute concentration (hypertonic). In this scenario, distilled water (0 M) is hypotonic to the solution inside the dialysis bag (0.5 M), causing water to enter the bag. This net flow of water into the bag will result in the bag swelling in size.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
