  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_biology_3101",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Three hundred college freshmen are observed to have grad point averages that are approximately normally distributed with mean 2.1 and a standard deviation of 1.2. How many of these freshmen would you expect to have grade point averages between 2.5 and 3.5 if the averages are recorded to the nearest tenth?\n\nOptions: \nA. 103 students\nB. 50 students\nC. 90 students\nD. 110 students\nE. 82 students\nF. 95 students\nG. 120 students\nH. 75 students\nI. 68 students\nJ. 57 students\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the expected number of students, we calculate the area under the normal distribution curve between $X = 2.45$ and $X = 3.55$ (adjusting for continuity since grades are recorded to the nearest tenth).\n1. Convert the bounds to Z-scores using $Z = \\frac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}$: $Z_1 = \\frac{2.45 - 2.1}{1.2} \\approx 0.29$ and $Z_2 = \\frac{3.55 - 2.1}{1.2} \\approx 1.21$.\n2. Using a standard normal table, $P(0.29 < Z < 1.21) = P(Z < 1.21) - P(Z < 0.29) \\approx 0.8869 - 0.6141 = 0.2728$.\n3. Multiply the probability by the total number of students: $300 \\times 0.2728 \\approx 81.84$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
