      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **A**.\n\n**Explanation:**\nInjecting antibodies *into* the egg (intracellularly) would not prevent fertilization because the ZP3 receptors are located on the outer surface (zona pellucida) of the egg. In contrast, blocking the external ZP3 proteins with antibodies (Option C) or pre-binding sperm receptors with purified ZP3 (Option B) would successfully inhibit the sperm-egg interaction. Option D is a valid observation because the specific binding occurs with ZP3, not ZP2.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
