    "global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2964",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The dorsal root of a nerve leading to the leg of a rat is severed. How will this affect the rat? How would the effect vary if the ventral root were severed instead?\n\nOptions: \nA. Both severing the dorsal and ventral roots cause increased sensation and reflex action.\nB. Severing the dorsal root causes loss of motor function, severing the ventral root causes enhanced sensation.\nC. Both severing the dorsal and ventral roots lead to complete paralysis of the affected part.\nD. Severing the dorsal root causes enhanced movement, severing the ventral root has no effect.\nE. Severing the dorsal root causes loss of sensation and reflex action, severing the ventral root causes inability to move the affected part.\nF. Severing the dorsal root has no effect, severing the ventral root causes loss of sensation.\nG. Severing the dorsal root causes paralysis, severing the ventral root causes heightened sensitivity.\nH. Severing the dorsal root causes involuntary twitching, severing the ventral root causes loss of reflex action.\nI. Severing the dorsal root causes increased reflex action, severing the ventral root causes loss of sensation.\nJ. Severing the dorsal root has no effect, severing the ventral root causes paralysis.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The dorsal root of a spinal nerve carries sensory information from the body to the spinal cord, while the ventral root carries motor commands from the spinal cord to the muscles. Therefore, severing the dorsal root results in a loss of sensation and sensory-dependent reflex arcs, whereas severing the ventral root leads to motor paralysis. This distinction is known as the Bell-Magendie law.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
