    "global index": "MMLUPro_biology_2946",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Can complete equilibrium in a gene pool exist in real situations?\n\nOptions: \nA. Genetic equilibrium can be achieved only through selective breeding\nB. Gene pool equilibrium is maintained exclusively by mutation\nC. Gene pool equilibrium is disrupted only by natural selection\nD. Only large populations can achieve genetic equilibrium\nE. Complete equilibrium in a gene pool is not expected\nF. Complete equilibrium in a gene pool can always be achieved\nG. Equilibrium can be achieved when there is no gene flow\nH. Equilibrium is guaranteed when there is a high mutation rate\nI. Complete equilibrium is possible if the population is isolated\nJ. Random reproduction is common in real situations\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "According to the Hardy-Weinberg principle, complete genetic equilibrium requires five specific conditions: no mutations, random mating, no natural selection, an infinitely large population size, and no gene flow. In real-world biological systems, it is virtually impossible for all these conditions to be met simultaneously and indefinitely. Therefore, evolution is constantly occurring, and complete equilibrium is a theoretical benchmark rather than a real-world occurrence.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
