    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_4408",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the maximum number of phases that can be at equilibrium with each other in a three component mixture?\n\nOptions: \nA. 6\nB. 9\nC. 2\nD. 4\nE. 10\nF. 8\nG. 7\nH. 3\nI. 1\nJ. 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the maximum number of phases at equilibrium in a multi-component system, we use Gibbs' Phase Rule: $F = C - P + 2$, where $F$ is the degrees of freedom, $C$ is the number of components, and $P$ is the number of phases.\n\nFor a three-component system ($C = 3$), the number of phases is maximized when the degrees of freedom ($F$) are at their minimum value of zero. Substituting these values into the formula gives $0 = 3 - P + 2$, which simplifies to $P = 5$.\n\nThe correct letter choice is \\boxed{J}.",
