  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_3633",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For a macroscopic object of mass $1.0 \\mathrm{{~g}}$ moving with speed $1.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}} / \\mathrm{{s}}$ in a one-dimensional box of length $1.0 \\mathrm{{~cm}}$, find the quantum number $n$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 8$10^{{26}}$\nB. 1$10^{{27}}$\nC. 1$10^{{26}}$\nD. 9$10^{{26}}$\nE. 6$10^{{26}}$\nF. 2$10^{{26}}$\nG. 4$10^{{26}}$\nH. 7$10^{{26}}$\nI. 5$10^{{26}}$\nJ.  3$10^{{26}}$\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the quantum number $n$ for a particle in a one-dimensional box, we use the formula for energy $E_n = \\frac{n^2h^2}{8mL^2}$ and the classical kinetic energy $E = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$. Equating these gives $n = \\frac{2mLv}{h}$.\n\nGiven values:\n$m = 1.0 \\text{ g} = 10^{-3} \\text{ kg}$\n$v = 1.0 \\text{ cm/s} = 10^{-2} \\text{ m/s}$\n$L = 1.0 \\text{ cm} = 10^{-2} \\text{ m}$\n$h \\approx 6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\text{ J}\\cdot\\text{s}$\n\nCalculating $n$:\n$n = \\frac{2 \\times (10^{-3} \\text{ kg}) \\times (10^{-2} \\text{ m}) \\times (10^{-2} \\text{ m/s})}{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\text{ J}\\cdot\\text{s}} = \\frac{2 \\times 10^{-7}}{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}} \\approx 3.018 \\times 10^{26}$\n\n\\boxed{J}",
