  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_chemistry_4070",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The vibrational wavenumber of $\\mathrm{{Br}}_2$ is $323.2 \\mathrm{{~cm}}^{{-1}}$. Evaluate the vibrational partition function explicitly (without approximation) and plot its value as a function of temperature. At what temperature is the value within 5 per cent of the value calculated from the approximate formula?\n\nOptions: \nA. 7000 K\nB. 2500 K\nC. 3500$\\mathrm{{~K}}$\nD. 6000 K\nE. 2000 K\nF.  4500$\\mathrm{{~K}}$\nG. 4000$\\mathrm{{~K}}$\nH. 5500 K\nI. 5000$\\mathrm{{~K}}$\nJ. 3000 K\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the temperature where the exact vibrational partition function $q_{\\text{vib}} = (1 - e^{-\\beta hc\\tilde{\\nu}})^{-1}$ is within 5% of the high-temperature approximation $q_{\\text{approx}} = \\frac{kT}{hc\\tilde{\\nu}}$, we set up the ratio $\\frac{q_{\\text{approx}}}{q_{\\text{vib}}} = 1.05$. \n\n1. The vibrational characteristic temperature $\\Theta_{\\text{vib}} = \\frac{hc\\tilde{\\nu}}{k} = 1.4388 \\text{ cm K} \\times 323.2 \\text{ cm}^{-1} \\approx 465 \\text{ K}$.\n2. Let $x = \\Theta_{\\text{vib}}/T$. The condition $\\frac{1/x}{1/(1-e^{-x})} = \\frac{1-e^{-x}}{x} = 0.95$ (or $1.05$ depending on the direction of error) yields a very small $x$ value, indicating the approximation is highly accurate even at moderate temperatures. \n3. Solving $\\frac{1 - e^{-x}}{x} \\approx 1 - \\frac{x}{2} = 0.95$ gives $x \\approx 0.1$, which corresponds to $T \\approx 4650 \\text{ K}$; looking at the provided options and more precise expansion, $T = 4500$ K is the standard result for this specific problem in physical chemistry texts (e.g., Atkins).\n\n\\boxed{F}",
      "success": true,
