    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A student performs five titrations and obtains a mean result of 0.110 M, with a standard deviation of 0.001 M. If the actual concentration of the titrated solution is 0.100 M, which of the following is true about the titration results?\n\nOptions: \nA. Moderately accurate and precise\nB. Accurate but not precise\nC. Precise but not accurate\nD. Both accurate and precise\nE. Neither precise nor slightly accurate\nF. Neither accurate nor precise\nG. Highly accurate but imprecise\nH. Accurate but imprecise\nI. Precise and slightly accurate\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct answer, we must evaluate both the precision and accuracy of the results:\n1.  **Precision** refers to the consistency of measurements; a standard deviation of 0.001 M relative to a mean of 0.110 M is very small, indicating the results are highly precise.\n2.  **Accuracy** refers to how close the mean is to the true value; the mean (0.110 M) deviates from the actual concentration (0.100 M) by 10%, which is a significant systematic error.\n\nSince the results are consistent but far from the truth, they are precise but not accurate.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
