  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7149",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose $\\Omega$ is a bounded open area in $\\mathbb{{R}}^n$. For any $f\\in L^2(\\Omega)$, the Laplace equation (with respect to a real function $u$), $\\Delta u = f$ with boundary condition $u\\mid_{{\\partial \\Omega}}=0$, has a unique weak solution. This can be proved by: 1. Poincare inequality and Riesz representation theorem; 2. Cauchy-Schwartz inequality and Hahn-Banach theorem. 3. None of the above. Return the answer as a number\n\nOptions: \nA. 2. Arzelà-Ascoli theorem and Banach fixed-point theorem\nB. 1. Arzelà-Ascoli theorem and Riesz representation theorem\nC. 1.0\nD. 3.0\nE. 2. Sobolev embedding theorem and Riesz representation theorem\nF. 1. Poincare inequality and Banach fixed-point theorem\nG. 2. Poincare inequality and Hahn-Banach theorem\nH. 2.0\nI. 1. Sobolev embedding theorem and Lax-Milgram theorem\nJ. All of the above\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The weak solution to the Poisson equation $\\Delta u = f$ with zero Dirichlet boundary conditions is typically found by applying the **Riesz Representation Theorem** (or the more general Lax-Milgram theorem) to the Hilbert space $H_0^1(\\Omega)$. To ensure that the bilinear form $\\int_\\Omega \\nabla u \\cdot \\nabla v \\, dx$ is coercive (equivalent to the $H^1$ norm), the **Poincaré inequality** is required. Thus, the proof relies on the Poincaré inequality and the Riesz representation theorem, which corresponds to option \"1\" in the question text.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
