    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "By the definition of a limit, $\\lim_{x \\to c} |f(x)| = 0$ if and only if for every $\\epsilon > 0$, there exists a $\\delta > 0$ such that $0 < |x - c| < \\delta$ implies $||f(x)| - 0| < \\epsilon$. Since $||f(x)|| = |f(x)| = |f(x) - 0|$, this is identical to the formal definition of $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = 0$. Therefore, if the limit of the absolute value of a function is zero, the limit of the function itself must also be zero.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
