    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For a parametric family $\\{{p_\\theta(x)\\}}$ we know that $\\lim_{{\\theta'\\to\\theta}}\\frac{{1}}{{(\\theta-\\theta')^2}}D(p_\\theta||p_{{\\theta'}}) = \\alpha J(\\theta)$, where $J(\\theta)$ is the Fisher information. Use natural logarithm for KL divergence to compute $\\alpha$.\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.25\nB. 4\nC. 0.5\nD. 1\nE. 2.5\nF. -1\nG. 2\nH. 1.5\nI. 3\nJ. 0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the value of $\\alpha$, we look at the second-order Taylor expansion of the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence $D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta'})$ around $\\theta' = \\theta$.\n\nFor a smooth parametric family, the expansion is given by:\n$D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta'}) \\approx D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta}) + \\frac{\\partial D}{\\partial \\theta'} \\bigg|_{\\theta'=\\theta} (\\theta' - \\theta) + \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{\\partial^2 D}{\\partial \\theta'^2} \\bigg|_{\\theta'=\\theta} (\\theta' - \\theta)^2$\nSince $D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta}) = 0$ and the first derivative at the minimum is 0, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{1}{2} J(\\theta) (\\theta' - \\theta)^2$, where $J(\\theta)$ is the Fisher information.\n\nComparing the limit $\\lim_{\\theta' \\to \\theta} \\frac{D(p_\\theta || p_{\\theta'})}{(\\theta - \\theta')^2} = \\frac{1}{2} J(\\theta)$ to the given $\\alpha J(\\theta)$, we find that $\\alpha = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.5$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
