    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To compute the integral $I=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{(x^2 + 1)^2} dx$, we can use the substitution $x = \\tan(\\theta)$, which gives $dx = \\sec^2(\\theta) d\\theta$. The integral becomes $\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sec^2(\\theta)}{(\\tan^2(\\theta) + 1)^2} d\\theta = \\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\cos^2(\\theta) d\\theta$, which simplifies to $\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\frac{1 + \\cos(2\\theta)}{2} d\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Since $\\pi \\approx 3.14159$, the result is $1.57079...$, which matches option G.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
