    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine if the polynomial $f(x) = x^2 - 12$ satisfies the Eisenstein criterion, we look for a prime $p$ such that $p$ divides the constant term ($-12$), $p$ divides all intermediate coefficients (none here), $p$ does not divide the leading coefficient ($1$), and $p^2$ does not divide the constant term.\n1. For $p=3$: $3 \\mid -12$, $3 \\nmid 1$, and $3^2 = 9 \\nmid -12$. This satisfies the criterion.\n2. For $p=2$: $2 \\mid -12$ and $2 \\nmid 1$, but $2^2 = 4 \\mid -12$, so the criterion is not satisfied for $p=2$.\n\nSince the criterion is satisfied for $p=3$, the polynomial is irreducible over $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
