    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7579",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $W(t)$ be a Bownian motion, Let $E[exp(i*W(t))]:= E[cos(W(t))+i*sin(W(t))]$, where $i=\\sqrt{{-1}}$. Is $M(t):=exp(i*W(t))/E[exp(i*W(t))]$ a matingale? Return 1 for yes and 0 for no.\n\nOptions: \nA. i (the imaginary unit)\nB. 1.0\nC. -0.5\nD. 2.0\nE. 1.5\nF. 0.0\nG. -2.0\nH. 3.0\nI. 0.5\nJ. -1.0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine if $M(t) = \\frac{\\exp(iW(t))}{E[\\exp(iW(t))]}$ is a martingale, we check the condition $E[M(t) | \\mathcal{F}_s] = M(s)$ for $s < t$. Using the independent increments property of Brownian motion, $E[\\exp(iW(t)) | \\mathcal{F}_s] = \\exp(iW(s)) E[\\exp(i(W(t)-W(s)))]$, and since $E[\\exp(iW(t))] = E[\\exp(iW(s))]E[\\exp(i(W(t)-W(s)))]$, the ratio satisfies the martingale property. Thus, the answer is \"yes,\" which corresponds to the numerical value 1.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
