      "generated_answer": "To find the integral $\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{dx}{1+x^2}$, we use the fundamental theorem of calculus and the fact that the antiderivative of $\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$ is $\\arctan(x)$.\n\n1.  Evaluate the antiderivative at the upper and lower limits:\n    $\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\frac{dx}{1+x^2} = [\\arctan(x)]_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}} = \\arctan(\\sqrt{3}) - \\arctan(0)$.\n2.  Recall the trigonometric values: $\\arctan(\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$ and $\\arctan(0) = 0$.\n3.  Calculate the numerical value: $\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\approx \\frac{3.14159}{3} \\approx 1.047197$.\n\nComparing this result to the given options, $1.0472$ matches option C.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
