    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7233",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose ${{X_n:n\\geq 1}}$ be independent and exponentially distributed with parameter 1. what is the probability $P(\\limsup _{{n \\rightarrow infty}} X_n/\\log(n)=1)? Return a numeric value.\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.5\nB. 1.0\nC. 0.1\nD. 0.36787944117 (1/e)\nE. 0.63212055882 (1 - 1/e)\nF. 0.845350936 (1 - 1/π)\nG. 0.0\nH. 0.69314718056 (1/ln(2))\nI. 0.9\nJ. 0.2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the probability $P(\\limsup_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} X_n/\\log(n)=1)$, we use the Borel-Cantelli Lemma. For independent $X_n \\sim \\text{Exp}(1)$, we have $P(X_n > c \\log n) = e^{-c \\log n} = n^{-c}$. \n\n1. If $c > 1$, $\\sum P(X_n > c \\log n) = \\sum n^{-c} < \\infty$, so $P(X_n > c \\log n \\text{ i.o.}) = 0$, meaning $\\limsup X_n/\\log n \\leq 1$ almost surely.\n2. If $c \\leq 1$, $\\sum P(X_n > c \\log n) = \\sum n^{-c} = \\infty$, and since the events are independent, $P(X_n > c \\log n \\text{ i.o.}) = 1$, meaning $\\limsup X_n/\\log n \\geq 1$ almost surely.\n\nThus, $\\limsup_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} X_n/\\log(n) = 1$ with probability 1.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
