    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the sum of $\\sum_{{n=1}}^{{\\infty}} \\frac{{2}}{{n^2 + 4n + 3}}$\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.6333\nB. 0.9333\nC. 2.0333\nD. 0.7333\nE. 0.8333\nF. 1.4333\nG. 2.1433\nH. 0.4333\nI. 0.5633\nJ. 1.2333\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the sum $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{2}{n^2 + 4n + 3}$, we first factor the denominator: $n^2 + 4n + 3 = (n+1)(n+3)$. Using partial fraction decomposition, we write $\\frac{2}{(n+1)(n+3)} = \\frac{1}{n+1} - \\frac{1}{n+3}$.\n\nThis results in a telescoping series:\nSum $= \\left(\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{4}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{5}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{6}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{7}\\right) + \\dots$\nMost terms cancel out, leaving: $\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{3+2}{6} = \\frac{5}{6} \\approx 0.8333$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
