    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The given equation $x^2 y'' - 2y = 0$ is a Cauchy-Euler equation with the characteristic equation $r(r-1) - 2 = 0$, which simplifies to $r^2 - r - 2 = 0$. The roots are $r_1 = 2$ and $r_2 = -1$, yielding the general solution $y(x) = c_1 x^2 + c_2 x^{-1}$. For the solution to remain bounded as $x \\rightarrow 0$, the coefficient of the singular term $x^{-1}$ must be zero ($c_2 = 0$), so $y(x) = c_1 x^2$; applying the initial conditions $y(1) = c_1 = 1$ and $y'(1) = 2c_1 = \\gamma$ results in $\\gamma = 2$.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
