      "generated_answer": "To find the limit, we first determine the expression for $F_n(x)$.\n1. $F_0(x) = \\log(x)$.\n2. $F_1(x) = \\int_0^x \\log(t) dt = x\\log(x) - x$.\n3. $F_2(x) = \\int_0^x (t\\log(t) - t) dt = \\frac{x^2}{2}\\log(x) - \\frac{3x^2}{4}$.\nBy induction, it can be shown that $F_n(x) = \\frac{x^n}{n!} \\left( \\log(x) - H_n \\right)$, where $H_n = \\sum_{k=1}^n \\frac{1}{k}$ is the $n$-th harmonic number.\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives $F_n(1) = \\frac{1}{n!} (0 - H_n) = -\\frac{H_n}{n!}$.\nThe expression to evaluate is $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{n! F_n(1)}{\\log(n)} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{-H_n}{\\log(n)}$. Since $H_n \\sim \\log(n) + \\gamma$, the limit is $-1$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
