    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7031",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is $\\lim _{{r \\rightarrow \\infty}} (\\int_0^{{\\pi/2}} x^r sin(x) dx)/(r\\int_0^{{\\pi/2}} x^r cos(x) dx)$?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.54321\nB. 0.22222\nC. 0.77777\nD. 0.87654\nE. 0.33333\nF. 0.99999\nG. 0.63662\nH. 1.23456\nI. 0.45678\nJ. 0.11111\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the limit $\\lim_{r \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx}{r \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx}$, we use integration by parts on the denominator. Let $I_D = \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\cos(x) dx$; by setting $u = \\cos(x)$ and $dv = x^r dx$, we get $I_D = \\left[ \\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1} \\cos(x) \\right]_0^{\\pi/2} + \\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1} \\sin(x) dx$. Evaluating the boundary terms gives 0, so the expression becomes $\\frac{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx}{r \\cdot \\frac{1}{r+1} \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^{r+1} \\sin(x) dx}$, and as $r \\to \\infty$, the ratio of the integrals $\\frac{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^r \\sin(x) dx}{\\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^{r+1} \\sin(x) dx}$ behaves like $1/(\\pi/2)$ while $r/(r+1) \\to 1$, resulting in $1/(\\pi/2) = 2/\\pi \\approx 0.63662$.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
