    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7254",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let I=[0,1]\\times[0,1]. Suppose $E={{(x, y) \\in I: sin(x)<\\frac{{1}}{{2}}, cos(x+y) is irrational}}$, what is the Lebesgue measure of E?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.7853981634\nB. 1.0471975512\nC. 1.8849555922\nD. 0.6283185307\nE. 0.2617993833\nF. 2.0943951024\nG. 1.5707963268\nH. 0.5235987667\nI. 0.3926990817\nJ. 1.3089969390\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the Lebesgue measure of $E$, we first consider the conditions defining the set $E \\subset [0,1] \\times [0,1]$.\n1. The condition $\\sin(x) < 1/2$ for $x \\in [0,1]$ corresponds to $0 \\le x < \\arcsin(1/2) = \\pi/6$. Since $\\pi/6 \\approx 0.5236$, the range for $x$ is $[0, \\pi/6]$.\n2. The condition that $\\cos(x+y)$ is irrational involves the set of rational values of the cosine function, which is countable; therefore, for any fixed $x$, the set of $y$ such that $\\cos(x+y)$ is rational has a Lebesgue measure of 0.\n3. Consequently, the measure of the set where $\\cos(x+y)$ is irrational is equal to the total area of the region defined by the $x$-constraint, which is the area of the rectangle $[0, \\pi/6] \\times [0, 1]$. This area is $(\\pi/6 - 0) \\times (1 - 0) = \\pi/6 \\approx 0.5235987756$.\n\nComparing this value to the given options, $0.5235987667$ is the closest approximation.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
