    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6502",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Does the following series $\\sum_{{i=0}}^{{\\infty}} \\frac{{n^2 ln(n)}}{{n!}}$ converge?\n\nOptions: \nA. It oscillates between values and does not settle\nB. 2.0\nC. -0.5\nD. It converges to e (Euler's number)\nE. -1.0\nF. 1.0\nG. It diverges to infinity\nH. 0.0\nI. It does not converge\nJ. It converges conditionally, not absolutely\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine if the series $\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{n^2 \\ln(n)}{n!}$ converges, we can apply the **Ratio Test**. \n\nLet $a_n = \\frac{n^2 \\ln(n)}{n!}$. We examine the limit of the ratio of successive terms:\n$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left| \\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} \\right| = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{(n+1)^2 \\ln(n+1)}{(n+1)!} \\cdot \\frac{n!}{n^2 \\ln(n)} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{(n+1)^2}{n^2(n+1)} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln(n+1)}{\\ln(n)} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{n+1}{n^2} \\cdot 1 = 0$$\nSince the limit is $0$, which is less than $1$, the series converges absolutely. Note that for series involving factorials in the denominator like $1/n!$, the growth of the factorial always dominates polynomials and logarithmic functions, ensuring convergence.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
