    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6426",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find all c in Z_3 such that Z_3[x]/(x^3 + x^2 + c) is a field.\n\nOptions: \nA. -2\nB. 2\nC. 1\nD. -1\nE. -3\nF. 0\nG. 3\nH. 4\nI. 6\nJ. 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine for which $c \\in \\mathbb{Z}_3$ the quotient ring $\\mathbb{Z}_3[x]/(x^3 + x^2 + c)$ is a field, we must find $c$ such that the polynomial $f(x) = x^3 + x^2 + c$ is irreducible over $\\mathbb{Z}_3$. Since $f(x)$ is a cubic polynomial, it is irreducible if and only if it has no roots in $\\mathbb{Z}_3 = \\{0, 1, 2\\}$.\n\n1. If $x=0$, $f(0) = c$. For $0$ not to be a root, $c \\neq 0$.\n2. If $x=1$, $f(1) = 1^3 + 1^2 + c = 2 + c$. For $1$ not to be a root, $2 + c \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}$, so $c \\neq 1$.\n3. If $x=2$, $f(2) = 2^3 + 2^2 + c = 8 + 4 + c = 12 + c \\equiv c \\pmod{3}$. For $2$ not to be a root, $c \\neq 0$.\n\nTesting $c=2$ (the only remaining value in $\\mathbb{Z}_3$): $f(0)=2$, $f(1)=1$, and $f(2)=2$. Since there are no roots, the polynomial is irreducible, making the quotient ring a field.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
