  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7574",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let M be the set of bounded functions (i.e. \\sup_{{x\\in[a,b]}}|f(x)|<\\infty) in C[0,1]. Is the set ${{F(x)=\\int_0^x f(t) dt | f \\in M }}$ a sequentially compact set? Answer 1 for yes and 0 for no. Furthermore, it can be proved using 1. Arzelà-Ascoli theorem, 2. Riesz representation theorem, 3. Banach fixed point theorem, 4. None of the above. Return the answers of the two questions in a list. For example, if you think the answer is no and Riesz representation theorem, then return [0,2].\n\nOptions: \nA. [0, 2]\nB. [0, 3]\nC. [1, 3]\nD. [0, 1]\nE. [1, 1]\nF. [1, 0]\nG. [1, 2]\nH. [0, 4]\nI. [1, 4]\nJ. [0, 0]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The set $M$ consists of functions $f \\in C[0, 1]$ that are bounded. Since any continuous function on a compact interval $[0, 1]$ is automatically bounded, $M$ is simply $C[0, 1]$. However, for the set of integrals $\\mathcal{F} = \\{F(x) = \\int_0^x f(t) dt \\mid f \\in M\\}$ to be sequentially compact in $C[0, 1]$, the set of derivatives $f$ must be uniformly bounded (i.e., $\\exists K$ such that $\\|f\\|_\\infty \\leq K$ for all $f$); without a global bound on $\\|f\\|_\\infty$, the set $\\mathcal{F}$ is not equicontinuous and thus not compact. Specifically, if $M$ is the entire space $C[0, 1]$, it is not a bounded set, so the resulting set of integrals is not relatively compact according to the Arzelà-Ascoli theorem. Therefore, the set is not sequentially compact (0), and the Arzelà-Ascoli theorem (1) is the standard tool used to analyze such compactness properties.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
