    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7451",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If $m$ and $n$ are odd integers, how many terms in the expansion of $(m+n)^6$ are odd?\n\nOptions: \nA. 6\nB. 3\nC. All the terms are odd\nD. 4\nE. 1\nF. 7\nG. 8\nH. 2\nI. 9\nJ. 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The general term in the expansion of $(m+n)^6$ is given by $T_{k+1} = \\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^k$ for $k = 0, 1, \\dots, 6$. Since $m$ and $n$ are odd, any power of $m$ or $n$ is also odd ($1^x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}$), so the parity of each term depends entirely on the binomial coefficient $\\binom{6}{k}$. Evaluating the coefficients $\\binom{6}{0}=1, \\binom{6}{1}=6, \\binom{6}{2}=15, \\binom{6}{3}=20, \\binom{6}{4}=15, \\binom{6}{5}=6, \\text{ and } \\binom{6}{6}=1$, we see that the odd values are 1, 15, 15, and 1. There are 4 such terms.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
