      "generated_answer": "To find the fundamental period, we examine the behavior of the function for several values of $n$:\n- For $n=0$: $f(x) = (-1)^0 = 1$ on $[-1, 0)$ and $f(x) = 1$ on $[0, 1)$. Thus, $f(x)=1$ for $x \\in [-1, 1)$.\n- For $n=1$: $f(x) = (-1)^1 = -1$ on $[1, 2)$ and $f(x) = 1$ on $[2, 3)$.\n- For $n=2$: $f(x) = (-1)^2 = 1$ on $[3, 4)$ and $f(x) = 1$ on $[4, 5)$. Thus, $f(x)=1$ for $x \\in [3, 5)$.\n- For $n=3$: $f(x) = (-1)^3 = -1$ on $[5, 6)$ and $f(x) = 1$ on $[6, 7)$.\n\nThe pattern of values for the intervals $[k, k+1)$ starting from $k=-1$ is: $1, 1, -1, 1, 1, 1, -1, 1 \\dots$. The sequence of values repeats every 4 units (e.g., the value $-1$ occurs on $[1, 2)$, then $[5, 6)$, etc.), making the fundamental period 4.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
