  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6719",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: for the matrix $A=(\\begin{{array}}{{rrrrr}} 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & -3 \\1 & 2 & 0 & -5 & 1 \\2 & 4 & -3 & -19 & 6 \\3 & 6 & -3 & -24 & 7\\end{{array}})$, what is its row rank and column rank? return the two numbers as a list.\n\nOptions: \nA. [1, 1]\nB. [3, 4]\nC. [4, 4]\nD. [2, 4]\nE. [2, 3]\nF. [3, 3]\nG. [4, 2]\nH. [2, 2]\nI. [4, 3]\nJ. [3, 2]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the row rank and column rank of the matrix $A$, we perform elementary row operations to find its row-echelon form:\n1. Let $R_1 = (1, 2, 3, 4, -3)$. Subtract $R_1$ from $R_2$: $R_2' = (0, 0, -3, -9, 4)$.\n2. Subtract $2R_1$ from $R_3$: $R_3' = (0, 0, -9, -27, 12)$. Note that $R_3' = 3R_2'$, so $R_3$ becomes a zero row.\n3. Subtract $3R_1$ from $R_4$: $R_4' = (0, 0, -12, -36, 16)$. Note that $R_4' = 4R_2'$, so $R_4$ becomes a zero row.\n\nSince there are only 2 non-zero rows in the row-echelon form, the row rank is 2. By the fundamental theorem of linear algebra, the row rank and column rank of any matrix are always equal, so the answer is [2, 2].\n\n\\boxed{H}",
