    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7107",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose the graph of f is both increasing and concave up on a <= x <= b. Then, using the same number of subdivisions, and with L, R, M, and T denoting, respectively, left, right, midpoint, and trapezoid sums, it follows that\n\nOptions: \nA. L <= T <= M <= R\nB. R <= M <= T <= L\nC. M <= L <= T <= R\nD. M <= R <= T <= L\nE. L <= M <= T <= R\nF. T <= L <= R <= M\nG. R <= L <= M <= T\nH. R <= T <= M <= L\nI. T <= R <= M <= L\nJ. L <= R <= T <= M\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct order, we analyze the properties of the integral approximations based on the function $f$ being increasing and concave up:\n1. Because $f$ is **increasing**, the Left sum $(L)$ underestimates and the Right sum $(R)$ overestimates the integral ($L \\leq \\text{Area} \\leq R$).\n2. Because $f$ is **concave up**, the Midpoint sum $(M)$ underestimates and the Trapezoid sum $(T)$ overestimates the integral ($M \\leq \\text{Area} \\leq T$).\n3. It is a known calculus property for concave functions that the error in the Trapezoid sum is approximately twice the error of the Midpoint sum in the opposite direction, placing the actual area between $M$ and $T$, and both $M$ and $T$ between $L$ and $R$.\n\nTherefore, the relationship is $L \\leq M \\leq \\text{Area} \\leq T \\leq R$.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
