  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7556",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Suppose $x-3$ and $y+3$ are multiples of $7$. What is the smallest positive integer, $n,$ for which $x^2+xy+y^2+n$ is a multiple of $7$?\n\nOptions: \nA. 9\nB. 8\nC. 5\nD. 6\nE. 3\nF. 2\nG. 4\nH. 7\nI. 10\nJ. 1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "From the problem, we have the following congruences modulo 7:\n1. $x - 3 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7} \\implies x \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}$\n2. $y + 3 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7} \\implies y \\equiv -3 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}$\n\nWe need to find the smallest positive integer $n$ such that $x^2 + xy + y^2 + n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}$. Substituting the values of $x$ and $y$:\n$x^2 + xy + y^2 + n \\equiv (3)^2 + (3)(4) + (4)^2 + n \\pmod{7}$\n$x^2 + xy + y^2 + n \\equiv 9 + 12 + 16 + n \\pmod{7}$\n$x^2 + xy + y^2 + n \\equiv 37 + n \\pmod{7}$\n\nSince $37 = 7 \\times 5 + 2$, we have $37 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}$.\nTherefore, $2 + n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}$.\nThe smallest positive integer $n$ that satisfies $n \\equiv -2 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}$ is $n = 5$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
