      "generated_answer": "To find the degree of the polynomial $h(x)$, let $d$ be the degree of $h$. We analyze the degrees of the three terms in the expression:\n1. The degree of $f(g(x))$ is $\\deg(f) \\cdot \\deg(g) = 6 \\cdot 3 = 18$.\n2. The degree of $g(h(x))$ is $\\deg(g) \\cdot \\deg(h) = 3 \\cdot d$.\n3. The degree of $h(f(x))$ is $\\deg(h) \\cdot \\deg(f) = d \\cdot 6 = 6d$.\n\nThe degree of the entire sum $f(g(x)) + g(h(x)) + h(f(x))$ is given as 36. Since $6d > 3d$ (for $d > 0$) and $6d$ must be at least as large as 18 to dominate the sum to reach 36, we set $6d = 36$, which yields $d = 6$.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
