    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7100",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following are valid intervals of convergence for a power series?\n(I) [−1, 3)\n(II) (−∞, 0]\n(III) [2, 2] ∪ [3, 3]\n(IV) (−∞,∞)\n\nOptions: \nA. Only (III)\nB. Only (II)\nC. (I), (III) and (IV)\nD. All of them\nE. (I) and (IV )\nF. (II) and (IV)\nG. Only (IV )\nH. (II), (III) and (IV)\nI. (I), (II) and (III)\nJ. (II) and (III)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "According to the theory of power series, the interval of convergence must be a single interval centered at some value $a$. Specifically, it can be a finite interval (open, closed, or half-open), the single point $\\{a\\}$ (where the radius $R=0$), or the set of all real numbers $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. \n\n- (I) $[ -1, 3)$ is a valid finite interval centered at $x=1$ with $R=2$.\n- (II) $(-\\infty, 0]$ is not possible because intervals of convergence must be symmetric around a center (except for the endpoints) or be the entire real line.\n- (III) $[2, 2] \\cup [3, 3]$ is not possible because the convergence set must be a single connected interval.\n- (IV) $(-\\infty, \\infty)$ is valid when the radius of convergence $R = \\infty$.\n\nThus, only (I) and (IV) are valid.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
