    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7240",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is 3^(3^(3^(...))) mod 100? There are 2012 3's in the expression.\n\nOptions: \nA. 87\nB. 07\nC. 77\nD. 47\nE. 57\nF. 27\nG. 37\nH. 17\nI. 67\nJ. 97\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find $3 \\uparrow \\uparrow 2012 \\pmod{100}$, we use the property that $3^x \\pmod{100}$ has a period related to $\\phi(100) = 40$. Since $\\gcd(3, 100) = 1$, we look at the exponent modulo $\\phi(100) = 40$, and subsequently the next exponent modulo $\\phi(40) = 16$, then $\\phi(16) = 8$, $\\phi(8) = 4$, and $\\phi(4) = 2$.\nFollowing the tower of powers, $3^{3^{\\dots}} \\pmod{40}$ eventually stabilizes because $3^4 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{80}$, and specifically $3^k \\pmod{40}$ results in $3 \\to 27 \\to 31 \\to 13 \\to 39 \\dots$; calculating $3^{3^3} \\equiv 3^{27} \\equiv 3^{27 \\pmod{16}} \\equiv 3^{11} \\equiv 7 \\pmod{40}$ leads to the evaluation $3^{40k + 7} \\pmod{100}$.\nCalculating $3^7 = 2187$, we find $2187 \\pmod{100} = 87$.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
