    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_6584",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What interval consists of all $u$ such that neither $2u$ nor $-20u$ is in the interval $(-\\infty,-1)$ ?\n\nOptions: \nA. [-2, -\\frac{{1}}{{2}}]\nB. [0, \\frac{{1}}{{2}}]\nC. [-\\frac{{1}}{{20}}, 0]\nD. [-\\frac{{1}}{{2}}, 0]\nE. [-1, 0]\nF. [\\frac{{1}}{{20}}, \\frac{{1}}{{2}}]\nG. [-1,1]\nH. [0,2]\nI. [1,2]\nJ. [-\\frac{{1}}{{2}}, -\\frac{{1}}{{20}}]\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the interval for $u$, we translate the condition that $2u$ and $-20u$ are not in $(-\\infty, -1)$ into inequalities:\n1. $2u \\notin (-\\infty, -1) \\implies 2u \\geq -1 \\implies u \\geq -\\frac{1}{2}$.\n2. $-20u \\notin (-\\infty, -1) \\implies -20u \\geq -1 \\implies u \\leq \\frac{1}{20}$.\n\nCombining these results gives the interval $[-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{20}]$. Among the provided options, the only interval that is a subset of this range and fits the logical structure of the problem is $[-\\frac{1}{2}, 0]$, as positive values up to $\\frac{1}{20}$ are included in the range but the specific constraint matches option D most closely.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
