    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_8274",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In a mixture of hydrogen, oxygen, and nitrogen gases at a given temperature, the fastest molecules on average are those of\n\nOptions: \nA. Nitrogen and hydrogen have the same average speed, but oxygen is slower\nB. nitrogen\nC. hydrogen\nD. The speed depends on the specific temperature and cannot be determined\nE. Nitrogen and oxygen have the same average speed, but hydrogen is slower\nF. Oxygen and hydrogen have the same average speed, but nitrogen is slower\nG. The speed of the gases is determined by their density, not their type\nH. All have same average speed\nI. The speed of the gases is independent of their type and depends solely on their volume\nJ. oxygen\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "According to the kinetic molecular theory, the average kinetic energy of gas molecules is the same for all gases at a given temperature ($KE = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$). Since kinetic energy is constant, the average speed of the molecules is inversely proportional to the square root of their molar mass ($v \\propto \\sqrt{1/M}$). Because hydrogen has the smallest molar mass ($H_2 \\approx 2$ g/mol) compared to nitrogen ($\\approx 28$ g/mol) and oxygen ($\\approx 32$ g/mol), its molecules move the fastest on average.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
