    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_9017",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If the Sun were suddenly replaced by a black hole of the same mass, it would have a Schwarzschild radius of 3,000 m. What effect, if any, would this change have on the orbits of the planets?\n\nOptions: \nA. The planets would be thrown out of the solar system.\nB. The planets would rotate more slowly.\nC. The planets would immediately be sucked into the black hole.\nD. The planets would move directly toward the Sun.\nE. The orbits would become circular.\nF. The planets would move in spiral orbits.\nG. The orbits would change based on the size of the planet.\nH. The orbits would become more elliptical.\nI. The planets would oscillate about their former elliptical orbits.\nJ. The orbits would remain unchanged.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The gravitational force between two objects is determined by their masses and the distance between them, as described by Newton's law of universal gravitation ($F = G \\frac{m_1 m_2}{r^2}$). Since the mass of the black hole is identical to the mass of the current Sun and the distance to the planets remains the same, the gravitational pull on the planets would not change. Therefore, the planets would continue to follow their current orbital paths.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
