    "global index": "MMLUPro_physics_8089",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What effect or effects would be most significant if the Moon's orbital plane were exactly the same as the ecliptic plane?\n\nOptions: \nA. Lunar eclipses would not last as long.\nB. Lunar eclipses would last much longer.\nC. Lunar eclipses would be much rarer.\nD. There would be no significant effect.\nE. Solar eclipses would last much longer.\nF. Solar eclipses would be much rarer.\nG. Solar eclipses would not last as long.\nH. Solar eclipses would be much more frequent.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "If the Moon's orbital plane were aligned exactly with the ecliptic plane, the Moon would pass through Earth's shadow (lunar eclipse) and block the Sun (solar eclipse) during every single full moon and new moon, respectively. Currently, these events are rare because the Moon's orbit is tilted about 5 degrees, causing it to usually pass above or below the alignment needed for an eclipse. Therefore, both solar and lunar eclipses would occur twice every lunar month instead of only a few times a year.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
