    "global index": "MathQA_1585",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: 50 men shake hands with each other . maximum no of handshakes without cyclic handshakes .\n\nOptions: \nA. 190\nB. 200\nC. 1225\nD. 220\nE. 230\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the maximum number of handshakes without any \"cyclic\" handshakes (forming a cycle like A-B, B-C, C-A), the handshakes must form a structure known in graph theory as a **tree**. In a tree involving $n$ vertices (men), the maximum number of edges (handshakes) possible without forming a cycle is always $n - 1$. For 50 men, this is $50 - 1 = 49$; however, since this value is not among the options and the phrasing often implies the total possible combinations in such problems, $50C2$ ($50 \\times 49 / 2$) equals 1225. Given the standard interpretation of handshake problems in competitive exams, 1225 is the total number of unique handshakes possible.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
