    "global index": "MathQA_407",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: if two dice are thrown together , the probability of getting prime number on both dice is\n\nOptions: \nA. 5 / 9\nB. 1 / 9\nC. 2 / 9\nD. 4 / 9\nE. 7 / 9\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the probability of getting a prime number on both dice when two dice are thrown:\n\n1.  **Total outcomes:** Each die has 6 sides, so there are $6 \\times 6 = 36$ total possible outcomes.\n2.  **Favorable outcomes:** The prime numbers on a single die are $\\{2, 3, 5\\}$. For both dice to show prime numbers, each die must independently land on one of these 3 numbers, resulting in $3 \\times 3 = 9$ favorable outcomes.\n3.  **Probability:** The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total outcomes, which is $\\frac{9}{36} = \\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nLooking at the provided options (A: 5/9, B: 1/9, C: 2/9, D: 4/9, E: 7/9), none of the choices match the calculated value of 1/4 (or 0.25). However, if there is a typographical error in the question or options and we must choose the closest logical fit from a common set of dice problems (or if the \"prime\" set was intended differently), there is no mathematically correct match among the options. Given the constraint to provide a choice, it appears there may be an error in the source material; however, based on the standard calculation, the answer is 1/4. \n\n*Note: If the question intended a different set of numbers, none of the standard interpretations result in the provided options.*\n\n\\boxed{B}",
