    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the Fourier cosine series for $f(x)$ on $[0, \\pi]$, we calculate the coefficients $a_0$ and $a_n$:\n1. $a_0 = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_0^\\pi f(x) dx = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_{\\pi/2}^\\pi 1 dx = \\frac{2}{\\pi} [\\frac{\\pi}{2}] = 1$. The first term is $\\frac{a_0}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n2. $a_n = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_{\\pi/2}^\\pi \\cos(nx) dx = \\frac{2}{\\pi} [\\frac{\\sin(nx)}{n}]_{\\pi/2}^\\pi = -\\frac{2}{n\\pi} \\sin(\\frac{n\\pi}{2})$.\n3. For $n$ even, $\\sin(\\frac{n\\pi}{2}) = 0$. For $n$ odd ($n=2k+1$), $\\sin(\\frac{(2k+1)\\pi}{2}) = (-1)^k$, leading to $a_{2k+1} = -\\frac{2}{\\pi} \\frac{(-1)^k}{2k+1}$, which matches option F.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
