    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{s+1}{s-1}\\right)$, we use the property $\\mathcal{L}\\{t f(t)\\} = -\\frac{d}{ds}F(s)$.\nDifferentiating $F(s) = \\ln(s+1) - \\ln(s-1)$ gives $F'(s) = \\frac{1}{s+1} - \\frac{1}{s-1}$, so $-F'(s) = \\frac{1}{s-1} - \\frac{1}{s+1}$.\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform of $-F'(s)$ yields $e^t - e^{-t} = 2\\sinh(t)$, and dividing by $t$ gives $f(t) = \\frac{2\\sinh(t)}{t}$.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
