    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the inverse Laplace transform $L^{-1}\\left[\\frac{1}{s(s^2 + 1)}\\right]$ using convolution, we identify the two functions as $F(s) = \\frac{1}{s}$ and $G(s) = \\frac{1}{s^2 + 1}$, which correspond to $f(t) = 1$ and $g(t) = \\sin t$. The convolution is calculated as $\\int_0^t f(u)g(t-u) du = \\int_0^t 1 \\cdot \\sin(t-u) du$. Evaluating the integral gives $[ \\cos(t-u) ]_0^t = \\cos(0) - \\cos(t) = 1 - \\cos t$.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
