    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10192",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For the zero-memory information source described by the table, find the average length (L_avg) of binary code needed to encode the symbols. Source symbol Symbol probability P_i s_1 (1 / 4) s_2 (1 / 4) s_3 (1 / 4) s_4 (1 / 4)\n\nOptions: \nA. 2 bits/symbol\nB. 2.2 bits/symbol\nC. 3.2 bits/symbol\nD. 1.5 bits/symbol\nE. 3 bits/symbol\nF. 1.75 bits/symbol\nG. 1 bit/symbol\nH. 4 bits/symbol\nI. 2.5 bits/symbol\nJ. 3.5 bits/symbol\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the average length ($L_{avg}$) for a zero-memory information source with equal probabilities, we first calculate the entropy ($H$), as the most efficient binary code for a source where all probabilities are powers of $1/2$ will satisfy $L_{avg} = H$.\n\n1.  The source has 4 symbols ($s_1, s_2, s_3, s_4$), each with a probability $P_i = 1/4$.\n2.  The entropy is calculated as $H = \\sum P_i \\log_2(1/P_i) = 4 \\times (1/4 \\log_2 4) = \\log_2 4 = 2$ bits/symbol.\n3.  Since each probability is $2^{-2}$, a fixed-length code of 2 bits (e.g., 00, 01, 10, 11) achieves this entropy exactly.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
