    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10243",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A phase-shift keying system suffers from imperfect synchronization. Determine \\textphi so that the P_e(PSK) =P_e(DPSK) = 10^-5\n\nOptions: \nA. 30^0\nB. 22^0\nC. 40^0\nD. 12^0\nE. 24^0\nF. 15^0\nG. 36^0\nH. 20^0\nI. 27^0\nJ. 18^0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the phase error $\\phi$ where the bit error probability of PSK with imperfect synchronization matches that of DPSK ($P_e = 10^{-5}$), we use the performance formulas:\n$P_e(\\text{DPSK}) = \\frac{1}{2} e^{-\\gamma_b}$ and $P_e(\\text{PSK}) = Q(\\sqrt{2\\gamma_b} \\cos \\phi)$.\n\nFor $P_e = 10^{-5}$, the required SNR for DPSK is approximately $\\gamma_b \\approx 10.3$ (or $10.1$ dB), while for ideal PSK ($\\phi=0$), it is $\\gamma_b \\approx 9.6$ (or $9.8$ dB). Solving the equation $Q(\\sqrt{2\\gamma_b} \\cos \\phi) = \\frac{1}{2} e^{-\\gamma_b}$ for the phase offset $\\phi$ yields a value of approximately $18^\\circ$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
