      "generated_answer": "To find the steady-state solution for the difference equation $y[n] + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}y[n-1] = \\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{6}n)$, we use the frequency response $H(e^{j\\omega}) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}e^{-j\\omega}}$.\nAt $\\omega = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$, $H(e^{j\\pi/6}) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{6} - j\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{6})} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} - j\\frac{1}{2})} = \\frac{1}{1.5 - j\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}}$.\nThe magnitude is $|H| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1.5^2 + (1/12)}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9/4 + 1/12}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{28/12}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}$, and the phase is $\\angle H = \\tan^{-1}(\\frac{1/(2\\sqrt{3})}{1.5}) = \\tan^{-1}(\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}})$, leading to $y_{ss}[n] = \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{6}n + \\tan^{-1}\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}})$.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
