    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10772",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let F= kr^nr̂ Evaluate \\int\\int_sF\\bullet n̂dsand \\int\\int_sF× n̂ds wheres is a sphere of radius a centered at the origin.\n\nOptions: \nA. 2\\pi k a^n+3, -1\nB. 4\\pi k a^n+2, 0\nC. 2\\pi k a^n+2, 0\nD. 6\\pi k a^n+2, \\pi\nE. 3\\pi k a^n+1, \\pi a^n\nF. 4\\pi k a^n+3, 1\nG. 4\\pi k a^n, 0\nH. 4\\pi k a^n-1, 0\nI. 2\\pi k a^n, 2\\pi a^n\nJ. 4\\pi k a^n+1, 0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To evaluate the surface integrals over a sphere of radius $a$ centered at the origin:\n\n1.  **For $\\iint_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} dS$:** On the surface of the sphere, $\\mathbf{r} = a \\hat{\\mathbf{r}}$, so $\\mathbf{F} = k a^n \\hat{\\mathbf{r}}$. The unit outward normal $\\hat{\\mathbf{n}}$ is equal to $\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}$, making $\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} = k a^n$. Integrating this constant over the surface area of the sphere ($4\\pi a^2$) gives $k a^n(4\\pi a^2) = 4\\pi k a^{n+2}$.\n2.  **For $\\iint_S \\mathbf{F} \\times \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} dS$:** Since $\\mathbf{F}$ is directed radially outward ($\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}$) and the normal $\\hat{\\mathbf{n}}$ is also radial ($\\hat{\\mathbf{r}}$), the cross product $\\mathbf{F} \\times \\hat{\\mathbf{n}} = (k a^n \\hat{\\mathbf{r}}) \\times \\hat{\\mathbf{r}} = \\mathbf{0}$. The integral of the zero vector over the surface is $0$.\n\nMatching these results to the options provided:\n\n\\boxed{B}",
