    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the initial value and slope of $f(t) = e^{-2t}u(t)$:\n1. **At $t = 0^+$:** $f(0^+) = e^{-2(0)} = 1$. The slope is the derivative $f'(t) = -2e^{-2t}$, so at $t = 0^+$, $f'(0^+) = -2$.\n2. **Initial-Value Theorem:** The Laplace transform is $F(s) = \\frac{1}{s+2}$. The initial value is $\\lim_{s \\to \\infty} sF(s) = \\lim_{s \\to \\infty} \\frac{s}{s+2} = 1$, and the initial slope is $\\lim_{s \\to \\infty} s[sF(s) - f(0^+)] = \\lim_{s \\to \\infty} s[\\frac{s}{s+2} - 1] = \\lim_{s \\to \\infty} \\frac{-2s}{s+2} = -2$.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
