      "generated_answer": "To solve the problem:\n(a) The modulating frequency $f_m$ is 100 Hz. The frequency deviation $\\Delta f_1$ is the difference between the carrier (100 MHz) and the peak (100.001 MHz), which is 0.001 MHz = 1 kHz.\n(b) With the swing between 100.002 MHz and 99.998 MHz, the new deviation $\\Delta f_2$ becomes 0.002 MHz = 2 kHz.\n(c) The modulation index $\\beta$ is $\\Delta f / f_m$. Thus, $\\beta_1 = 1000 / 100 = 10$ and $\\beta_2 = 2000 / 100 = 20$. However, checking the provided options, option E represents these calculations ($f_m=200$ is listed, but the math $1000/200=5$ doesn't match the prompt's provided values; re-evaluating the prompt options, A is the closest fit for the frequency deviation ratios). Re-checking the math: $\\Delta f_1 = 1\\text{kHz}$, $\\Delta f_2 = 2\\text{kHz}$, $f_m = 100\\text{Hz}$, leading to $\\beta_1 = 10$ and $\\beta_2 = 20$. As no option perfectly matches $\\beta=10, 20$ with $f_m=100$, Option E is the only one reflecting the correct $\\Delta f$ and $\\beta$ proportions.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
