    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the general solution of the differential equation $\\frac{d^2x}{dt^2} - 2\\frac{dx}{dt} - 3x = 10\\mathbf{a} \\sin t + \\mathbf{b}(2t + 1)$, we first solve the characteristic equation $r^2 - 2r - 3 = 0$, which yields $r = -1$ and $r = 3$, giving the complementary solution $x_c = c_1e^{-t} + c_2e^{3t}$. \n\nTesting the particular solution for the trigonometric part $x_{p1} = \\mathbf{a}(A \\cos t + B \\sin t)$, we substitute into the differential equation to find constants that satisfy $-4A - 2B = 0$ and $2A - 4B = 10$, leading to $A = 1$ and $B = -2$, hence $\\mathbf{a}(\\cos t - 2 \\sin t)$. \n\nFor the polynomial part $x_{p2} = \\mathbf{b}(Ct + D)$, substitution yields $-3Ct - (2C + 3D) = 2t + 1$, resulting in $C = -2/3$ and $D = -1/9$, which simplifies to $-\\mathbf{b}[\\frac{2}{3}t + \\frac{1}{9}]$. Combining these parts matches Option B.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
