    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Calculate the electrostatic energy of a system consisting of a point charge q located at the center of a spherical conducting shell of inner radius r_1 and outer radius r_2.\n\nOptions: \nA. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q / r^2)\nB. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (1/4) q^2 {{(1 / r_1) - (1 / r_2)}}\nC. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q / r_1) - (q / r_2)\nD. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (1/2) q^2 {{(1 / r_1) - (1 / r_2)}}\nE. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (1/2) q^2 {{(1 / r^2) - (1 / r_1)}}\nF. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} q^2 {{(r_2 - r_1) / (r_1 r_2)}}\nG. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q^2 / r_1)\nH. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q^2 / r_2)\nI. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (q^2 / r_1) - (q^2 / r_2)\nJ. {{1 / (4\\pi\\epsilon_0)}} (1/2) q^2 {{(1 / r_1) + (1 / r_2)}}\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the electrostatic energy of the system, we consider the electric field $\\mathbf{E}$ in the different regions: for $r < r_1$, $E = \\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r^2}$; for $r_1 < r < r_2$, $E = 0$ (inside the conductor); and for $r > r_2$, $E = \\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r^2}$. The total energy $W = \\frac{\\epsilon_0}{2} \\int E^2 dV$ is the sum of the energy from $0$ to $r_1$ and from $r_2$ to $\\infty$, but since the question typically asks for the interaction energy or the change in energy involving the shell boundaries relative to a point charge in vacuum, we evaluate the integral difference or the work required to assemble the configuration. The term $\\frac{1}{8\\pi\\epsilon_0} q^2 (\\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_2})$ represents the energy stored specifically within the field \"excluded\" by the conductor.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
