    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Using the following data for cadmium, determine the absoluteis entropy ofCdat 298°K. Assume that T^3 law isvalid below 12°C and also, there is no difference between C_p andC_vat these low temperatures. Data C_p, cal / deg / g-atom C_p, cal / deg / g-atom °^T_K, cadmium °^T_K, cadmium 12 0.392 130 5.608 14 .592 140 5.684 16 .804 150 5.746 18 1.020 160 5.799 20 1.240 170 5.844 25 1.803 180 5.884 30 2.306 190 5.922 35 2.760 200 5.956 40 3.158 210 5.988 45 3.503 220 6.018 50 3.803 230 6.047 60 4.283 240 6.073 70 4.647 250 6.096 80 4.920 260 6.119 90 5.138 270 6.144 100 5.284 280 6.171 110 5.413 290 6.201 120 5.518 298.16 6.224\n\nOptions: \nA. 12.186 cal / deg / g-atom\nB. 8.186 cal / deg / g-atom\nC. 20.186 cal / deg / g-atom\nD. 11.086 cal / deg / g-atom\nE. 16.186 cal / deg / g-atom\nF. 14.186 cal / deg / g-atom\nG. 15.086 cal / deg / g-atom\nH. 22.186 cal / deg / g-atom\nI. 10.186 cal / deg / g-atom\nJ. 18.186 cal / deg / g-atom\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the absolute entropy ($S_{298}$) of Cadmium, we calculate the integral $S = \\int_{0}^{298.16} \\frac{C_p}{T} dT$. This is done in two parts: (1) from 0 K to 12 K using the Debye $T^3$ law ($S_{12} = \\frac{C_{p, 12}}{3} = \\frac{0.392}{3} \\approx 0.131$), and (2) from 12 K to 298.16 K by numerically integrating the provided $C_p$ data (using the trapezoidal rule or Simpson's rule for $\\frac{C_p}{T}$ vs. $T$). Summing the initial contribution and the integral of the experimental data yields a value of approximately 12.186 cal/deg/g-atom.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
