    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the correction for compressibility, we compare the actual speed ($V$) with the indicated speed ($V_i$) derived from the Bernoulli equation for incompressible flow ($\\Delta p = \\frac{1}{2} \\rho V_i^2$). For an airspeed of 300 mph (approximately Mach 0.4 at sea level), the compressibility effect causes the stagnation pressure to be slightly higher than the incompressible prediction, meaning the instrument overreads the true airspeed. A common rule of thumb or calculation using the expansion of the compressible pressure equation shows that at 300 mph, the indicated speed is about 5 mph higher than the actual speed, requiring a correction of -5 mph.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
