    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10943",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For a single tone-modulated signal m(t) =cos\\omega_mt Find the demodulated single sideband outputg(t).\n\nOptions: \nA. y(t) = (Ar)cos(2ω_mt±Δφ)\nB. y(t) = (Ar/ 3)cos(ω_mt±3Δφ)\nC. y(t) = (Ar/ 4)cos(ω_mt±Δφ)\nD. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)cos(ω_mt±Δφ/2)\nE. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)sin(ω_mt)\nF. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)sin(2ω_mt±Δφ/2)\nG. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)cos(ω_mt±Δφ)\nH. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)sin(ω_mt±Δφ)\nI. y(t) = (Ar/ 2)cos(ω_mt)\nJ. y(t) = (Ar)cos(ω_mt±Δφ/3)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In the coherent demodulation of a Single Sideband (SSB) signal, the received signal is multiplied by a local carrier $r(t) = A \\cos(\\omega_c t \\pm \\Delta\\phi)$, where $\\Delta\\phi$ represents the phase error. For a modulating signal $m(t) = \\cos(\\omega_m t)$, the SSB signal (e.g., USSB) is $\\cos(\\omega_c + \\omega_m)t$; after multiplication and low-pass filtering, the output is scaled by a factor of $1/2$ due to product-to-sum identities. This results in the demodulated output $y(t) = \\frac{Ar}{2} \\cos(\\omega_m t \\pm \\Delta\\phi)$, indicating that a phase error causes a phase shift in the recovered message.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
