  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10537",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the energy stored in an inductor from t = 0 to t = 1 whose \\textphi -icharacteristic is given by \\textphi (t) = 1/3i^3 (t) and the current through it is given by i (t) = 2 sin t.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.00 J\nB. 0.75 J\nC. 1.50 J\nD. 4.00 J\nE. 2.50 J\nF. 3.00 J\nG. 1.25 J\nH. 2.75 J\nI. 2.00 J\nJ. 3.50 J\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the energy stored in the inductor, we use the formula for energy $W = \\int i \\, d\\phi$. Given $\\phi = \\frac{1}{3}i^3$, the differential $d\\phi = i^2 \\, di$, so the integral becomes $W = \\int i \\cdot i^2 \\, di = \\int i^3 \\, di = \\frac{1}{4}i^4$.\n\nAt $t = 1$, the current is $i(1) = 2 \\sin(1)$. Evaluating the energy at this point (assuming the energy at $t=0$ is zero because $i(0) = 2\\sin(0) = 0$):\n$W = \\frac{1}{4}(2 \\sin(1))^4 = \\frac{16 \\sin^4(1)}{4} = 4 \\sin^4(1)$.\nUsing the approximation $\\sin(1) \\approx 0.84147$, we calculate $4 \\times (0.84147)^4 \\approx 4 \\times 0.5017 \\approx 2.0068$ J.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
