    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A single phase full bridge inverter can operated in load commutation mode in case load consist of\n\nOptions: \nA. Purely resistive load.\nB. RL.\nC. RLC underdamped with a parallel connection.\nD. RLC critically damped.\nE. RLC underdamped with a series connection.\nF. Capacitive load.\nG. RLC underdamped.\nH. Purely inductive load.\nI. RLC overdamped.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To achieve load commutation in a thyristor-based inverter, the load current must lead the output voltage so that the load capacitance can provide the necessary reverse bias to turn off the thyristors. This condition is met when the load is an **RLC underdamped** circuit (specifically a series connection) operating at a frequency higher than the resonant frequency, resulting in a leading power factor. Among the provided options, G and E describe this state, but G is the most standard general classification for this mode of operation.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
