    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 44-year-old female presents to the office for evaluation of a lump on her neck that she noted 1 week ago. She denies any tenderness, fever, weight loss, or fatigue. Physical examination reveals a 2-cm freely movable mass in the lower left lobe of the thyroid. In addition to thyroid-stimulating hormone and free thyroxine levels, the most appropriate initial method to investigate this lesion is\n\nOptions: \nA. Thyroid biopsy\nB. Positron emission tomography (PET) scan\nC. Lymph node biopsy\nD. Computed tomography (CT) scan\nE. an iodine131 scan\nF. a nuclear thyroid scan\nG. Endoscopic examination of the throat\nH. fine-needle aspiration\nI. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)\nJ. ultrasonography of the thyroid gland\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most appropriate initial step in evaluating a palpable thyroid nodule, alongside biochemical testing (TSH), is **ultrasonography of the thyroid gland** to characterize the nodule's features and determine the need for further intervention. While fine-needle aspiration (FNA) is the definitive diagnostic tool, ultrasound is performed first to confirm the presence of the nodule, assess its size, and identify suspicious sonographic patterns that guide the decision to biopsy. Therefore, according to standard clinical guidelines (such as those from the ATA), ultrasound is the first-line imaging modality.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
