    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5297",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 35-year-old female develops nausea and vomiting after she underwent a cholecystectomy for protracted ileus. Her body mass index was 23 kg/m2 before surgery, and, except for the cholecystitis, she was in good health. The patient remains unable to eat 6 days after surgery. Which of the following forms of nutritional support is most appropriate for this patient?\n\nOptions: \nA. oral protein-rich diet\nB. feeding through a nasogastric feeding tube\nC. feeding through a jejunostomy tube\nD. central hyperalimentation\nE. intravenous saline solution\nF. peripheral hyperalimentation\nG. oral carbohydrate-rich diet\nH. feeding through a gastrostomy tube\nI. oral fat-free modular diet\nJ. intravenous dextrose solution\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **D. central hyperalimentation** (also known as Total Parenteral Nutrition or TPN).\n\nIn a post-surgical patient with a BMI of 23 who has been unable to eat for 6 days due to a protracted ileus, nutritional support is indicated because the gut cannot be used (ruling out enteral and oral options). For a patient expected to require prolonged nutritional support (exceeding 7 days) without a functional GI tract, central hyperalimentation is the standard of care to prevent protein-calorie malnutrition. Intravenous dextrose or saline alone provides insufficient caloric and nutritional support for surgical recovery.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
