    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely location for fluid to accumulate following the rupture of an ectopic pregnancy in the ampulla is the **Pouch of Douglas** (rectouterine pouch). This is the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity in a woman when standing or supine, serving as a collection point for free intraperitoneal fluid or blood. The ampulla is an intraperitoneal structure, so its rupture leads to hemoperitoneum rather than containment within the mesometrium or uterine cavity.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
