    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_4953",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 30-year-old woman, gravida 2, para 0, aborta 1, at 28 weeks' gestation comes to the office for a prenatal visit. She has had one previous pregnancy resulting in a spontaneous abortion at 12 weeks' gestation. Today, her vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows a uterus consistent in size with a 28-week gestation. Fetal ultrasonography shows a male fetus with no abnormalities. Her blood group is O, Rh-negative. The father's blood group is B, Rh-positive. The physician recommends administration of Rho\n\nOptions: \nA. Development of polycythemia\nB. Formation of antibodies to RhB\nC. Development of lymphocytosis\nD. Formation of antibodies to RhO\nE. immune globulin to the patient. This treatment is most likely to prevent which of the following in this mother?\nF. Development of leukocytosis\nG. Formation of antibodies to RhD\nH. Development of anemia\nI. Formation of antibodies to RhC\nJ. Development of natural killer cells\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **G**.\n\nThe patient is Rh-negative (O-negative) and is at 28 weeks' gestation, which is the standard time to administer Rho(D) immune globulin to prevent Rh alloimmunization. This treatment works by clearing any fetal Rh-positive red blood cells from the maternal circulation, thereby preventing the mother from forming her own antibodies against the RhD antigen (anti-D antibodies). This prevention is crucial for protecting future pregnancies from hemolytic disease of the newborn.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
