    "global index": "MMLUPro_health_5123",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 19-year-old male presents to the office for evaluation after he was hit from behind below the right knee while playing football. Gait analysis reveals a lack of fluid motion. Standing flexion test results are negative. Cruciate and collateral knee ligaments appear intact. Foot drop on the right is noted. The most likely diagnosis is\n\nOptions: \nA. meniscus tear\nB. tibial stress fracture\nC. posterior cruciate ligament tear\nD. unilateral sacral shear\nE. patellar dislocation\nF. medial collateral ligament sprain\nG. posteriorly deviated fibular head\nH. anterior cruciate ligament tear\nI. plantar flexed cuboid\nJ. anteriorly deviated distal femur\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The patient's presentation of a blow to the lateral aspect of the leg below the knee (the fibular head) resulting in \"foot drop\" indicates an injury to the common peroneal (fibular) nerve. A blow to the posterior aspect of the proximal fibula causes it to move anteriorly, but a blow from behind that results in nerve compression typically involves a positional dysfunction of the fibular head. In osteopathic clinical scenarios, a \"posteriorly deviated fibular head\" is the classic associated finding that compresses the common peroneal nerve against the fibular neck, leading to weakness in dorsiflexion (foot drop).\n\n\\boxed{G}",
