    "global index": "MedMCQA_1582",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: When a person lies down :\n\nOptions: \nA. There is immediate increase in venous return\nB. Heart rate becomes stable at slightly more than normal\nC. Cerebral blood flow increases & becomes stable at more than normal\nD. Blood flow to the Apex of lung decreases\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **A**.\n\nWhen a person moves from a standing to a lying position, gravity no longer causes blood to pool in the lower extremities, leading to an immediate increase in venous return to the heart. This increase in preload temporarily raises stroke volume and activates the baroreceptor reflex to regulate heart rate and blood pressure. Conversely, blood flow to the apex of the lung increases (rather than decreases) due to the removal of vertical gravitational gradients.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
