    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A patient presented with acute exacerbation of bronchial asthma. Salbutamol inhalation didn't improve the condition of the patient. So, intravenous coicosteroids and aminophylline were added and the condition improved. What is the mechanism of action of coicosteroids in this condition?\n\nOptions: \nA. They cause bronchodilatation when given with xanthines\nB. They increase bronchial responsiveness to salbutamol\nC. They increase the action of aminophylline on adenosine receptors\nD. They increase the mucociliary clearance\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Corticosteroids improve responsiveness in acute asthma primarily by upregulating and restoring the sensitivity of beta-2 adrenergic receptors, which enhances the effect of medications like salbutamol. They also reduce airway inflammation and edema, though the specific synergy mentioned in this clinical scenario focuses on reversing beta-receptor down-regulation. Therefore, the most accurate mechanism among the options provided is the restoration of bronchial responsiveness to sympathomimetics.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
