    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following hormones increases the sensitivity of heart to epinephrine:\n\nOptions: \nA. Parathyroid\nB. Insulin\nC. Thyroid\nD. Glucagon\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Thyroid hormones (specifically T3 and T4) increase the expression of beta-adrenergic receptors in the heart, making it more sensitive to the effects of epinephrine and norepinephrine. This phenomenon is known as the \"permissive effect\" of thyroid hormones on catecholamines. Consequently, excess thyroid hormone can lead to symptoms like tachycardia and increased cardiac output.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
