    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 27 year primigravida presents with pregnancy induced hypertension with blood pressure of 150/100 mm of Hg at 32 weeks of gestation with no other complications. Subsequently, her blood pressure is controlled on treatment. If there are no complications, the pregnancy should be terminated at:\n\nOptions: \nA. 40 completed weeks\nB. 37 completed weeks\nC. 35 completed weeks\nD. 34 completed weeks\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "According to current obstetric guidelines (such as ACOG and NHBPEP), for a patient with gestational hypertension or mild preeclampsia without severe features that is well-controlled, delivery is recommended at 37 0/7 weeks of gestation. Terminating the pregnancy at 37 weeks balances the risks of continuing the pregnancy (such as progression to eclampsia or placental abruption) against the risks of prematurity. Therefore, if the blood pressure is controlled and no other complications arise, delivery should not be delayed beyond 37 weeks.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
