    "global index": "MedMCQA_1612",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If the distribution of intraocular pressure (IOP) seen in 100 glaucoma patients has an average 30 mm with an SD of 1, what is the lower limit of the average that can be expected 95% of times -\n\nOptions: \nA. 28\nB. 26\nC. 32\nD. 25\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the lower limit of the average (mean) that can be expected 95% of the time, we calculate the 95% Confidence Interval using the formula: $\\text{Mean} \\pm 1.96 \\times (\\text{SD} / \\sqrt{n})$. \n\n1.  Standard Error (SE) = $1 / \\sqrt{100} = 0.1$.\n2.  The 95% Confidence Interval = $30 \\pm (1.96 \\times 0.1)$, which is $30 \\pm 0.196$.\n3.  The lower limit is approximately 29.8; however, based on the options provided which likely assume the calculation for the **population distribution** (Mean $\\pm$ 2 SD) rather than the confidence interval of the mean, $30 - 2(1) = 28$.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
