    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following structures is not of ectodermal origin:\n\nOptions: \nA. Hunter-schreger bands\nB. Enamel spindles\nC. Enamel tufts\nD. Enamel lamellae\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "All the listed structures (Hunter-Schreger bands, enamel spindles, enamel tufts, and enamel lamellae) are histological features found within the enamel, which is an ectodermally derived tissue. However, **enamel spindles** are technically formed when odontoblastic processes (of mesenchymal/ectomesenchymal origin) become trapped in the early enamel matrix before the dentinoenamel junction is fully established. While they are found *in* the enamel, their cellular origin is the dental papilla (mesenchyme), unlike the other structures which are purely products of the enamel organ.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
