    "global index": "MedMCQA_2301",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Cell membrane of a cell is freely permeable to three ions X, Y and Z. At resting membrane potential, the respective equilibrium potentials of Xand Y are -30mV and -40 mV.What will happen to the membrane potential if a drug is infused which can block the permeability of Z?\n\nOptions: \nA. Depolarisation\nB. Hyperpolarization\nC. No change\nD. Hyperpolarization following repolarization\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the effect on the membrane potential, we must consider the contribution of each ion to the resting membrane potential ($V_m$). Since the membrane is freely permeable to all three ions, the $V_m$ must lie between the equilibrium potentials of the ions; given $E_X$ is -30 mV and $E_Y$ is -40 mV, the presence of ion Z must be responsible for maintaining the potential at a typical resting level (usually around -70 mV), implying $E_Z$ is significantly more negative. If the permeability of the hyperpolarizing ion Z is blocked, the membrane potential will move toward the more positive equilibrium potentials of X and Y, resulting in a less negative state.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
