      "generated_answer": "The context indicates that using prescription data to estimate asthma prevalence (the method used by Stock et al.) resulted in extremely high peaks in infants (11.7–17.7%) that did not align with clinical survey data (0–2.6%). This discrepancy suggests that prescription-based criteria likely overestimate asthma in young children by including transient respiratory issues. Therefore, the study implies that prescriptions are not a reliable proxy for childhood asthma.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
