  {
    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_191",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following scales or chords can be played on the harp using a glissando technique?\n①C whole tone scale\n②Ab Ionian mode\n⓷a minor harmonic D-VII7 chord\n④G major D7 chord\n⑤Chromatic scale\n\nOptions: \nA. ①②③\nB. ①②③④\nC. ①③④\nD. ②③④⑤\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct answer, one must consider the pedal mechanism of the concert harp, which allows for seven strings per octave (C, D, E, F, G, A, B) to be tuned individually to flat, natural, or sharp positions. \n\n1.  **① C whole tone scale:** Possible by tuning strings to C, D, E, F#, G#, A#, and B# (enharmonically C).\n2.  **② Ab Ionian mode:** Possible as it is a standard diatonic scale (Ab, Bb, C, Db, Eb, F, G).\n3.  **⓷ a minor harmonic D-VII7 chord:** This refers to the diminished seventh chord on the leading tone (G#-B-D-F); possible by using enharmonic tunings to cover all seven strings (e.g., G#, B, D, F, Ab, Cb, E#).\n4.  **④ G major D7 chord:** Possible by tuning strings to the chord tones (D, F#, A, C) and using enharmonic equivalents for the remaining strings (e.g., Gb, B#, Eb) to avoid dissonant notes.\n5.  **⑤ Chromatic scale:** **Impossible** via glissando because the harp only has seven strings per octave and pedals cannot be shifted fast enough during a single stroke to produce all twelve semitones.\n\nBased on this, items ①, ②, ③, and ④ are possible.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
