    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is the correct for the inversion of the augmented sixth with ♭E as the root note?\n\nOptions: \nA. Its inverted equivalent interval is a major second\nB. The solfege name after inversion is ♯C-♭E\nC. The inverted property is a minor third\nD. The inverted property is a major second\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the correct answer, we first identify the original interval: an augmented sixth starting from ♭E is ♭E to C♯.\n\n1.  **Inversion of Interval Type:** The inversion of an \"augmented\" interval is always \"diminished,\" and the inversion of a \"sixth\" is a \"third\" (9 - 6 = 3). Therefore, the inversion is a **diminished third**.\n2.  **Solfege/Notes:** Inverting the interval ♭E-C♯ results in C♯-♭E.\n3.  **Enharmonic Equivalence:** A diminished third (C♯ to ♭E) is enharmonically equivalent to a **major second** (e.g., C to D or C♯ to D♯). \n\nOption D is the most accurate description of the property of the inverted interval in standard music theory contexts.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
