    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: How many diatonic semitones appear in the score example L:1/8\nM:none\nK:C\nB,^B,^C=C _D^E^F=E |] %1?\n\nOptions: \nA. 3\nB. 2\nC. 1\nD. 4\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the number of diatonic semitones, we must identify intervals between adjacent notes that have different letter names and represent a half-step distance.\n\n1.  **B, to ^B,**: Chromatic (same letter).\n2.  **^B, to ^C**: Diatonic (B# to C# is a whole step).\n3.  **^C to =C**: Chromatic (same letter).\n4.  **=C to _D**: **Diatonic semitone** (C to Db is a half step).\n5.  **_D to ^E**: Diatonic (Db to E# is an augmented second).\n6.  **^E to ^F**: Diatonic (E# to F# is a whole step).\n7.  **^F to =E**: **Diatonic semitone** (F# to E is a whole step, but F# to E natural in reverse is a major second; however, in the sequence ^F to =E, it is a whole step). \n\nRe-evaluating the specific half-steps:\n- C to Db (Notes 4 and 5) is a diatonic semitone.\n- E# to F# (Notes 6 and 7) is a diatonic semitone.\n\nThere are 2 diatonic semitones in the sequence.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
