      "generated_answer": "To determine which case involves parallel fifths or octaves, we analyze the intervals between the notes in the provided ABC notation (where the first note in brackets is the lower voice and the second is the upper voice):\n\n*   **Option A (Key C):** The first chord is [E-B] (a perfect fifth); the second chord is [D-d] (an octave). This is a motion from a fifth to an octave, but not parallel.\n*   **Option B (Key C):** [E-B] (perfect fifth) moves to [G-d] (perfect fifth). This is a **parallel fifth** (E to G in the bass and B to d in the soprano).\n*   **Option C (Key F):** [E-B] (diminished fifth, because B is flat in the key of F) moves to [D-d] (octave).\n*   **Option D (Key F):** [A,-B] (major second/ninth) moves to [D-d] (octave).\n\nOption B shows a shift from one perfect fifth (E-B) to another perfect fifth (G-d) moving in the same direction, which constitutes parallel fifths.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
