      "generated_answer": "To determine the number of double diminished intervals downwards from F♯ within an octave, we examine each scale degree:\n1.  **Double diminished 5th (dd5):** A perfect 5th down from F♯ is B; a diminished 5th is B♯; a double diminished 5th is **B𝄪**.\n2.  **Double diminished 4th (dd4):** A perfect 4th down from F♯ is C♯; a diminished 4th is C𝄪; a double diminished 4th is **C♯♯♯** (practically non-existent in standard theory).\n3.  **Double diminished octave (dd8):** An octave down from F♯ is F♯; a diminished octave is F𝄪; a double diminished octave is **F♯♯♯**.\nIn standard common-practice music theory, only the double diminished 5th (F♯ to B𝄪) is typically recognized as a functional interval within an octave.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
