      "generated_answer": "In music theory, particularly regarding strictly diatonic mirror inversion (melodic inversion), using the **IIIº (mediant)** as the axis for inversion results in a change of the tonal structure (the arrangement of tones and semitones), as the new scale would typically shift into a different mode (e.g., Ionian becoming Phrygian). Conversely, using the IIº (re) as the axis preserves the interval quality perfectly because the scale structure is symmetrical around that point. Therefore, the statement claiming the IIIº preserves the same tonal structure is incorrect.\n\nThe correct answer is \\boxed{C}.",
