    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_285",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following is a descending natural minor scale with E as the leading tone?\n\nOptions: \nA. L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:C\n ^F,4 | ^E,4 | =E,4 | ^D,4 | =D,4 | ^C,4 | ^B,,4 | =B,,4 | ^A,,4 | =A,,4 | ^G,,4 | ^F,,4 |] %12\nB. L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:A\n f4 | e4 | _e4 | d4 | _d4 | c4 | B4 | _B4 | A4 | _A4 | G4 | _G4 | F4 |] %13\nC. L:1/4\nM:4/4\nK:F\n f4 | e4 | _e4 | d4 | _d4 | c4 | B4 | A4 | _A4 | G4 | _G4 | F4 |] %12\nD. None of the first three options are correct\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct answer, we must identify the natural minor scale where **E** functions as the **leading tone**.\n\n1.  **Analyze the leading tone:** In a natural minor scale, there is technically no \"leading tone\" (a half-step below the tonic) as found in harmonic minor; instead, the seventh degree is a \"subtonic\" (a whole-step below). However, if E is the seventh degree of a natural minor scale, the tonic (the first note) would be **F** (as F natural minor uses E-flat, and F# natural minor uses E-natural).\n2.  **Evaluate F# Natural Minor:** The F# natural minor scale consists of F#, G#, A, B, C#, D, E, F#. Descending, this is: F#, E, D, C#, B, A, G#, F#.\n3.  **Examine the options:** \n    *   **Option A** starts on F# and descends chromatically, which is not a natural minor scale.\n    *   **Option B** is a chromatic descent from f to F.\n    *   **Option C** is also a chromatic descent.\n    *   Since none of the ABC notations represent a simple 8-note natural minor scale (they all contain 12 or 13 chromatic steps), none of the first three options are correct.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
