      "generated_answer": "To determine the inversion of a diminished fourteenth, we first simplify the interval: a diminished fourteenth ($d14$) is a compound diminished seventh ($d7 + \\text{octave}$). According to the rules of interval inversion, a diminished interval becomes augmented, and the sum of the interval numbers must equal 9 (for simple intervals) or 16/23 (for compound intervals). Therefore, a $d14$ inverts to an augmented second ($A2$), and its compound equivalents include an augmented ninth ($A9$) or an augmented second separated by an octave; an augmented seventh is the inversion of a diminished second and does not correspond to a diminished fourteenth.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
