      "generated_answer": "To determine which method keeps the notes in the same physical position on the staff, we compare the original pitch (G major on bass clef) to the resulting positions:\n\n*   **A:** Moving a minor second requires shifting the note positions up or down.\n*   **B:** In the alto clef, the middle line is C; shifting a G major melody (where G is the top space of the bass clef) to F major (where F would occupy that same space) results in a different pitch relationship.\n*   **C:** Changing to treble clef while transposing to D major moves the notes significantly on the staff.\n*   **D:** In the bass clef, the top space is G; in the treble clef, the bottom line is E. If you move a melody down a major second (G to F) and switch to treble clef, the note F (the new tonic) occupies the space just below the bottom line, which does not align with the original visual positions. \n\nHowever, re-evaluating **B**: In the bass clef, the fourth line is F. In the alto clef, the third (middle) line is C, making the fourth line E and the space above it F. If a melody starts on G (top space, bass clef) and transposes to F major (top space, alto clef), the physical position remains the same.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
